WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.760
How can a cell destroy a membrane-spanning receptor without leaving part of it

2
00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:08.640
behind? In chapter one, the receptor escaped.

3
00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.500
It released its LDL in acid and left before the end.

4
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.920
But some receptors are meant to be destroyed.

5
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.280
Many activated growth-factor receptors are like this, shown here in sky blue.

6
00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:30.460
Activation can add ubiquitin, a small protein shown in green.

7
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.680
The tagged receptors enter an early endosome.

8
00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:38.900
Ubiquitin helps mark them for lysosomal degradation.

9
00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:41.470
Here is the problem.

10
00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:47.710
The receptor sits in the endosome's own membrane, with its tail out in the cytosol.

11
00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:52.430
Suppose this endosome simply fused with a lysosome.

12
00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:56.130
Could the enzymes inside reach the whole receptor?

13
00:01:01.833 --> 00:01:08.333
Not all of it. The enzymes work in the lumen, so only the part facing the lumen is

14
00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:09.333
within their reach.

15
00:01:10.393 --> 00:01:14.353
The cell gets around this by moving the receptor off that outer membrane.

16
00:01:17.433 --> 00:01:22.753
From the early endosome on, patches of its outer membrane, the limiting membrane,

17
00:01:23.073 --> 00:01:24.453
bud inward into the lumen.

18
00:01:25.753 --> 00:01:28.473
Each bud pinches off as a small vesicle inside.

19
00:01:29.813 --> 00:01:33.113
An endosome full of them is a multivesicular body.

20
00:01:34.167 --> 00:01:36.687
What decides which proteins go into the buds?

21
00:01:38.407 --> 00:01:43.067
Complexes called ESCRT, in coral, recognize the ubiquitin tag.

22
00:01:44.327 --> 00:01:47.127
They sort the tagged receptors into the inner vesicles.

23
00:01:48.487 --> 00:01:51.887
In this comparison, an untagged protein stays behind.

24
00:01:54.967 --> 00:01:58.667
The multivesicular body then fuses with a lysosome.

25
00:01:59.887 --> 00:02:04.887
Each inner vesicle now floats whole in the lumen, receptor and all, and the enzymes

26
00:02:04.887 --> 00:02:09.347
digest it. This is how those receptors are switched off.

27
00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.720
So the endosome sorts more than cargo.

28
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.500
A receptor left in the limiting membrane can still return.

29
00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:24.380
A ubiquitin tag can select a receptor for inward budding.

30
00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:28.060
The lysosome can then digest all of it.

31
00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.780
Even a looping receptor can be pulled out of its loop.

32
00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:39.840
PCSK9, in cyan, is a protein that cells secrete.

33
00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:44.940
It binds one small part of the LDL receptor's outer region.

34
00:02:45.983 --> 00:02:48.963
Cultured human cells show what happens next.

35
00:02:50.203 --> 00:02:55.123
A receptor bound this way goes to the lysosome instead of returning to the surface.

36
00:02:56.543 --> 00:02:58.503
Its loop has become a one-way trip.

37
00:02:59.723 --> 00:03:02.143
The receptor does not come back to grip again.

38
00:03:05.167 --> 00:03:08.607
So a cell can destroy a receptor, not just its cargo.

39
00:03:09.967 --> 00:03:14.767
A ubiquitin tag can select the receptor for inward budding at the endosome.

40
00:03:16.067 --> 00:03:20.527
Inward budding puts the whole receptor within lysosomal enzymes' reach.
